---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';

const title = 'About';
const description = `Meet ${SITE.author}, learn why ${SITE.name} exists, and see how every article is researched and reviewed.`;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="post author-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Behind AI Pickle</p>
    <h1>AI advice for regular people, not a benchmark leaderboard.</h1>

    <div class="author-summary">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the author</p>
      <p class="post-dek">
        Hi, I'm {SITE.author}. I work a regular office job, use AI tools in day-to-day work,
        and started {SITE.name} to answer a practical question: is a new tool genuinely
        useful, or is it mostly marketing?
      </p>
      <p>
        My perspective is deliberately practical rather than academic. I care about the
        moment after the demo: whether a tool fits a real workflow, saves meaningful time,
        and earns the money or attention it asks for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What I look for</h2>
    <p>
      I am not an engineer grading model architecture. I evaluate tools from a working
      user's point of view: how quickly they become useful, what they cost, where the
      workflow breaks, and whether the result is worth switching tools or paying for
      another subscription.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Usefulness:</strong> what problem the tool solves and who benefits.</li>
      <li><strong>Friction:</strong> setup, learning curve, integrations, limits, and failure modes.</li>
      <li><strong>Value:</strong> what the cost buys compared with existing tools or a manual workflow.</li>
      <li><strong>Honesty:</strong> what is verified, what is reported by the vendor, and what still needs testing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How articles are made</h2>
    <p>
      Research, first drafts, and image generation are supported by an automated AI
      pipeline. Nothing is meant to go live without a human review. Before publication, I
      check the source, remove unsupported claims, improve unclear English, and make sure
      the article does not imply hands-on testing that did not happen.
    </p>
    <p>
      When an article includes first-hand results, it says exactly what was tested. When
      it summarizes public reporting or product documentation, the primary source is
      linked near the top of the article. Corrections are welcome and are reflected in an
      updated date when they materially change a post.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read a review</h2>
    <p>
      Articles are labeled by evidence. A hands-on result describes what was actually tested.
      A source-based analysis explains a product announcement, study, or public report without
      presenting it as independent testing. A practical guide focuses on a repeatable way for
      readers to make their own comparison.
    </p>
    <p>
      Product features, prices, and availability can change quickly. Check the linked primary
      source and the publication or update date before making a purchase or operational decision.
    </p>

    <h2>Independence and advertising</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} does not currently run display ads or affiliate links. If that changes,
      commercial relationships will be clearly disclosed and will not determine the
      conclusion of a review.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and feedback</h2>
    <p>
      If you spot an incorrect claim, outdated product detail, broken source, or unclear
      description of testing, please send the specific article and correction through the
      <a href={withBase('/contact')}> contact page</a>. Substantive corrections are reflected
      in the article's updated date.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Have a correction, a tool worth investigating, or a question? Use the
      <a href={withBase('/contact')}> contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
